Chapter: CharLitProbe

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The five inputs from findings/charlit/README.md, one per line, each a
 character literal that is NOT correctly terminated. Every one of them should
 raise a lexical diagnostic and today only the first two do.

 EACH LINE IS ITS OWN CASE and they are deliberately not combined, because the
 failure mode swallows the rest of the line: `'ab'` emits a CharLiteral over
 `'a`, leaves `b`, and then the trailing quote OPENS A NEW LITERAL that eats
 whatever comes next. Two cases on one line would measure the first one's
 damage rather than the second one's behaviour.

 THIS FILE IS NOT EXPECTED TO COMPILE. It is a lexer input; the point is which
 diagnostics come back, not whether a program runs. Read the diagnostics, not
 the exit code.

Section: Cases

 Case 1 -- quote at end of input. Caught today: ErrorToken.
 Case 2 -- backslash then end of input. Caught today: ErrorToken.

 Case 3 -- a character then end of LINE, no closing quote. SILENT today:
 CharLiteral of length 2.
  case-3 : Integer
  case-3 = 'a

 Case 4 -- a quote then end of line. SILENT today, and the NEWLINE becomes the
 character.
  case-4 : Integer
  case-4 = '

 Case 5 -- two characters in one literal. SILENT today, and the worst of them:
 a CharLiteral over `'a`, then `b` as an identifier, then a stray `'` that
 opens a fresh literal running to the next quote or the end of the line.
  case-5 : Integer
  case-5 = 'ab'
